# Locale, units and time

Use the person’s own Home Assistant language, units and time settings. UK English is the style of this documentation, not an instruction to change a household’s language, units or clock. Keep each value and its meaning, and format it using the native configuration.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant profile settings supply the localisation preferences. The civica Energy tables already read the native number format, clock format and time zone. This page covers other surfaces and does not claim they all have an adapter. For the native settings, see the Home Assistant [user configuration documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/configuration/user-configuration/).

The examples are simulated. They do not show that a native feature or adapter has been installed. An application that follows this page must pass the acceptance checks below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Measured value | The quantity and unit together, in native formatting. | Turn a rate into a total, or add precision the source does not have. |
| Date-only item | The calendar date, with no invented midnight time. | Shift an all-day task to another date by converting time zones. |
| Timed observation | What the time means (observation or period) and the time zone that applies. | Treat `last_changed` alone as a new measurement time. |
| Repeated local time | The offset or other context that makes it clear. | Show “01:30” alone, because it can mean two different moments. |
| Unknown value or translation | Plain text saying it is missing, and any name exactly as the person typed it. | Substitute zero or quietly translate an identifier. |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native locale, number separators, units and 12 or 24 hour setting. Do not take them from the chosen civica palette or only from the browser language.
2. Keep date-only values, instants, durations and recurring local schedules as different data types. Convert only when the source calls for it.
3. At a daylight-saving change, tell equal clock times apart by their offset or by the whole period. Keep the original dates of a comparison series and do not relabel them as today.
4. Isolate identifiers inside right-to-left text. Mirror the layout where it suits, but do not reverse physical directions, media, units or chart meaning automatically.

## Accessible interaction

- Let long translations and enlarged text wrap without clipping names, units, field errors or the main action.
- Make the spoken value include its unit and any time context. Do not rely on an abbreviation to explain an unfamiliar quantity.
- Avoid numeric dates that can be read two ways in authored examples. Mark an illustrative translation as unreviewed unless a suitable language review exists.

## Failure and recovery

- If a locale formatter fails, keep the source value and unit with a plain fallback. Do not show NaN or an empty value as if it had worked.
- If the person changes a preference, reformat what is shown. Do not change a stored measurement, a schedule instant or an identifier.
- If the time zone is unknown, say so. Do not assume Europe/London. An example with a stated zone does not set the household’s real zone.

## Complete examples

The examples use invented records. Forms check input and show local feedback only. They do not call Home Assistant, save preferences or claim a result on a real device. Links in a specimen lead to the explanation in the same specimen.

